//! # Ownership and sending
//!
//! Ownership tracks values that must have one responsible owner. Most Hew data
//! behaves like ordinary data: reusing it can create an independent logical
//! value. Resources, stream halves, tasks, generators, some closures, and
//! aggregates containing them transfer instead.
//!
//! ```hew
//! type Reading {
//!     label: string;
//!     samples: Vec<i64>;
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let original = Reading { label: "cpu", samples: [70, 80] };
//!     var revised = original;
//!     revised.samples.push(90);
//!     println(original.samples.len());
//!     println(revised.samples.len());
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `original` and `revised` are independent values. An implementation may
//! share immutable storage internally, but mutation preserves this observable
//! value behavior.
//!
//! ## Bindings are not ownership modes
//!
//! `let` makes a binding immutable. `var` permits reassignment, field or
//! element assignment, and calls to `var self` methods. Both bindings can hold
//! ordinary data or an affine owner. The value's type and the operation decide
//! whether a second use copies, borrows, or transfers it.
//!
//! An ordinary function parameter borrows or copies according to its type.
//! `consume value: T` says the callee needs an owned value. `consume self`
//! ends ownership of a method receiver. For ordinary copyable data the caller
//! may still keep an independent value; an affine owner moves and cannot be
//! reused from its previous place.
//!
//! ## Value and owner categories
//!
//! | Category | Examples | Reuse and cleanup |
//! | --- | --- | --- |
//! | Ordinary data | numbers, strings, bytes, records, enums, collections | Independent value behavior; recursive automatic cleanup |
//! | Actor reference | a named actor type or `actor(M) -> R` | Copies the actor identity; `stop` requests termination |
//! | Affine resource | files, sockets, stream halves, `#[resource]` values | Transfers ownership; scope exit closes a live owner |
//! | Linear value | `#[linear]` declarations | Transfers ownership; every path must explicitly consume it |
//! | Task or generator | `Task<T>`, generator handles | Transfers ownership; the enclosing construct settles cleanup |
//! | Callable | closures and function values | Copies or transfers according to captured owners and callable capabilities |
//!
//! Aggregates inherit their members' obligations. A record containing a stream
//! half is affine; a collection of linear values must eventually move each
//! element to a consuming operation.
//!
//! ## Sending values to actors {#send}
//!
//! `Send` means that a value can cross an actor boundary without exposing
//! shared mutable state. The compiler derives this property; user code does
//! not implement a `Send` trait or add a `#[send]` marker.
//!
//! Primitive values, strings, actor references, and records, enums, tuples,
//! options, results, arrays, and collections whose members are Send can cross
//! the boundary. Ordinary data arrives as an independent logical snapshot.
//! An admitted affine owner transfers to the receiver. Raw foreign pointers,
//! task and generator handles, and values whose safety cannot be established
//! are rejected.
//!
//! ```hew
//! type Job { id: i64; note: string; }
//!
//! actor Worker {
//!     receive fn run(job: Job) -> i64 { job.id }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let worker = spawn Worker;
//!     let job = Job { id: 42, note: "index" };
//!     println(worker.run(job).expect("worker completes the job"));
//!     println(job.note);
//!     stop(worker);
//!     stopped(worker);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! The caller can still use `job` because ordinary message data is
//! snapshotted. Sending an affine resource instead transfers its sole owner.
//! The same Send check applies to actor call parameters, mailbox submissions,
//! replies, and values captured by a lambda actor.
//!
//! ## Loans and collection access
//!
//! Reading from a collection may temporarily lend an element rather than copy
//! it when the element has no copy operation. While that loan is live, the
//! collection cannot be mutated or transferred. Hew infers these loans; source
//! code has no lifetime annotations.
//!
//! Use [resources](language.resources.html) for deterministic cleanup,
//! [actors](language.actors.html) for delivery and isolation, and
//! [functions](language.functions.html) for receiver and callable ownership
//! contracts. For a more gradual introduction, read the
//! [ownership concepts guide](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts/ownership).
